---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/environmental-science/foundational/ecosystems-energy">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is one of the opening lessons of Environmental Science — its sibling lesson traces
    the carbon cycle and climate. It assumes nothing except everyday arithmetic (multiply,
    divide, take a percentage) and an interest in where food comes from. Everything else is
    built here, step by step.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>define an ecosystem and state its two governing rules — matter cycles, energy flows — and say why they are different rules;</li>
    <li>trace energy from sunlight through producers, herbivores and predators, and explain what happens to the roughly 90% lost at every step;</li>
    <li>distinguish gross from net primary productivity, and read the map of where the planet's biomass is actually made;</li>
    <li>explain why food chains rarely run past four or five links, and why big fierce animals are rare;</li>
    <li>carry out the three standard field techniques — quadrats, transects and mark–recapture — including their arithmetic and their pitfalls;</li>
    <li>connect the ten-percent rule to land use, including the hidden cost of the food on your plate.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit: whenever you meet an organism, ask what supports it from below. Every level
    of life is paid for by the level beneath it — and tracing those payments is what this
    lesson teaches.
  </p>

  <h2>Why big fierce animals are rare</h2>
  <p>
    Sit in a meadow on a summer day and count what you see. Millions of insects. Dozens of
    birds. Perhaps a single kestrel, if the meadow is lucky. You will never see a kestrel
    hunting another kestrel, and no meadow on Earth could feed a population of wolf-sized
    hunters. The ecologist Paul Colinvaux made this puzzle famous: <em>why are big fierce
    animals so rare?</em> The answer is a piece of arithmetic so consequential that it shapes
    nearly everything else in ecology — and this lesson builds it from the ground up.
  </p>
  <p>
    First, the setting. An <strong>ecosystem</strong> is a community of organisms plus the
    physical world they inhabit: the <em>biotic</em> part (plants, animals, fungi, microbes)
    and the <em>abiotic</em> part (sunlight, water, soil, temperature) bound into one working
    system. A rocky tide pool is an ecosystem; so is the Amazon. Two rules govern every one
    of them. <em>Matter cycles</em>: carbon, nitrogen and water pass from soil to plant to
    animal and back, endlessly. <em>Energy flows</em>: it enters once, as sunlight, and
    leaks away at every step as heat, never to return.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="file each process by which rule it obeys" widget="sort-buckets" config={ecoRulesBuckets} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> an ecosystem runs on two kinds of traffic. Matter recirculates
    like water in a sealed pipe network; energy moves like a conveyor that only travels
    upward and bleeds at every station. Now we follow the conveyor from sunlight to kestrel.
  </p>

  <h2>Producers: the gateway from sunlight to life</h2>
  <p>
    Every energy story in biology starts with <strong>producers</strong> — plants, algae and
    some bacteria — because they alone can convert sunlight into stored chemical energy by
    <strong>photosynthesis</strong>: carbon dioxide and water, powered by light, become sugar
    and oxygen. Plants join sugar units into cellulose for structure and starch for storage.
    But the gateway is narrow: of all the solar energy striking a leaf, only about 1% ends up
    captured in sugars. The rest is reflected, transmitted, or spent warming and cooling the
    plant.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="call the capture — the sunlight gateway" widget="estimate" config={ecoGatewayEstimate} />
  <p>
    Plants also run the reaction in reverse. <strong>Respiration</strong> burns stored sugar
    with oxygen to power the plant's own life — day and night — releasing carbon dioxide,
    water and heat. Growth happens only while photosynthesis out-earns respiration, and the
    surplus after the plant has paid itself is what every animal on Earth eventually lives on.
  </p>
  <p>
    Sunlight is not the only gateway. Around deep-sea hydrothermal vents, where no light
    reaches, <strong>chemosynthetic</strong> bacteria build sugars from inorganic molecules
    such as hydrogen sulfide — entire ecosystems powered by chemistry instead of the Sun.
    Rare, but they reveal the real job description: capture energy from the non-living world,
    by whatever means.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> a plant's mass comes mostly from the soil it grows in —
  that soil is "eaten" and converted into wood and leaves. It seems reasonable: plants stand
  in soil, and farmers enrich it. But nearly all dry biomass is built from carbon dioxide
  pulled from the air and water drawn from the ground; soil supplies only small quantities
  of minerals. The 17th-century botanist van Helmont grew a willow for five years in about
  90 kg of soil and found the soil had lost barely 60 grams while the tree had gained about
  74 kilograms. This is also why productivity is measured as carbon fixed from the air, not
  soil consumed.</p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="bet before you read on — where a tree's mass comes from" widget="predict" config={ecoWillowPredict} />

  <h2>The leaky pipeline: energy climbs the trophic levels</h2>
  <p>
    Ecologists sort an ecosystem's residents into <strong>trophic levels</strong> — feeding
    steps. Producers occupy the base. Above them, <strong>consumers</strong>: herbivores eat
    producers (primary consumers), carnivores eat herbivores (secondary consumers), carnivores
    eat other carnivores (tertiary consumers), up to the <strong>apex consumers</strong> at
    the top. Off to the side runs the recycling crew: <strong>decomposers</strong> — bacteria
    and fungi — and <strong>detritivores</strong> such as earthworms and woodlice, which feed
    on dead matter and return its molecules to the soil.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="pair each role with its place in the pipeline" widget="match" config={ecoRolesMatch} />
  <p>
    At every handoff up the chain, about 90% of the energy is lost. Where does it go? Burned
    in movement and body heat; voided in excretion; locked in indigestible parts — cellulose,
    bone, hide — that the eater cannot break down; and a large share passes sideways to the
    decomposers. The physics behind this is not negotiable. The first law of thermodynamics
    says energy is never created or destroyed, only changed in form — so the lost energy is
    not gone. The second law says every conversion degrades some of it to heat that disperses
    and can no longer be gathered to power anything. Biology cannot repeal either law; it can
    only live inside them.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="find the faulty line — where the 90% goes" widget="spot-error" config={ecoLossSpot} />
  <p>
    What survives the losses is captured in a rule of thumb: roughly a tenth of the energy
    at one trophic level reaches the level above. The curve below starts with 100 units of
    producer energy and follows it up the chain.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="why there are no wolf-eating dragons — energy at each level, on the ten-percent rule" widget="plotter" config={tenPctCfg} />

  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the collapse is brutal, and the curve's shape is the
    point. From 100 units at the producers, herbivores receive about 10, second-level
    consumers about 1, third-level consumers 0.1, fourth-level consumers 0.01. Now answer
    the meadow question: a kestrel hunting three steps above the plants is spending energy
    that has already been taxed 90% three times over. That is why big fierce animals are
    rare, why food chains rarely run past four or five links, and why a tiger's territory
    spans hundreds of square kilometres — it lives at the far end of a pipeline that
    forfeits nine-tenths of everything at every station.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> sunlight enters through a 1% gateway, and each animal step
    forfeits about 90% of what it receives. The numbers explain the shape of life itself.
    Next, how ecologists put real, measured figures on the pipeline.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> energy cycles through an ecosystem the way carbon and
  nitrogen do — that the heat "lost" at each stage somehow loops back to the producers. The
  idea seems plausible precisely because matter does exactly that, and because we are taught
  that nature "recycles everything". But every energy transfer ends as heat that disperses
  and becomes unavailable for work. An ecosystem is an open system for energy — it needs a
  continuous input of sunlight or it stops — and effectively closed for matter. "Energy
  flows" and "matter cycles" are stated as two rules because they are two genuinely
  different behaviours.</p>

  <h2>Measuring the flow: GPP, NPP and the Silver Springs audit</h2>
  <p>
    Ecologists keep two ledgers at the pipeline's base. <strong>Gross primary
    productivity</strong> (GPP) is the total energy producers fix by photosynthesis.
    <strong>Net primary productivity</strong> (NPP) is what remains after the producers pay
    their own respiration — the actual surplus available to every other level. The
    distinction matters because plants are not charities. In the classic study of the
    Silver Springs ecosystem in Florida, producers captured 20,810 kilocalories per square
    metre per year — but nearly two-thirds of that was spent running the plants themselves,
    leaving about 7,600 for everyone else.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="fill the blanks — gross vs net" widget="cloze" config={ecoLedgerCloze} />
  <p>
    NPP is the most consequential number in ecosystem ecology, and it varies by more than
    two orders of magnitude across the planet, set by sunlight, water and nutrients:
    tropical rainforest fixes around 2,200 grams of biomass per square metre per year,
    desert under 100, and the open ocean a thin 125 — though spread across three quarters
    of the planet's surface. The chart compares classic values:
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="net primary productivity by biome — where the planetary surplus is actually made" widget="chart" config={nppCfg} />

  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the bars sort into two worlds. On land, productivity
    follows water and warmth — rainforest at the top, desert at the bottom. The open
    ocean's low bar hides its global weight: multiplied over its enormous area, that thin
    per-square-metre trickle still makes the ocean a major share of all photosynthesis on
    Earth. (Phytoplankton alone perform roughly 40% of it.) When humans convert high-NPP
    land such as forest and grassland, they are spending from the richest accounts in the
    planetary budget.
  </p>
  <p>
    The trophic handoffs have been measured, not just modelled. Howard Odum's audit of
    Silver Springs: producers fixed 20,810 kcal per m² per year; herbivores received 3,368
    (about 16% — a generous pipeline); small carnivores 383; top carnivores just 21.
    Decomposers processed 5,060 — more than the herbivores — a reminder that the
    "invisible" end of the pipeline handles the bulk of the flow. Pyramids pile up
    everywhere as a consequence: of energy, of standing biomass, and usually of numbers.
    (The one famous inversion: in the open ocean, the phytoplankton biomass at any instant
    is smaller than the krill eating it, because phytoplankton divide so fast that they are
    a tiny reservoir with a huge throughput — energy pyramids still stand; only the
    snapshot biomass topples.)
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="stack Odum's Silver Springs audit" widget="order" config={ecoAuditOrder} />

  <h2>Forest ecosystems: three designs for one job</h2>
  <p>
    Forests are the planet's great energy collectors, but the three main kinds run opposite
    strategies on the same sunlight. <strong>Tropical rainforest</strong> is a skyscraper:
    with light and water abundant, the canopy layers itself — emergents, canopy,
    understorey, dark floor — and over half of all land species live in it. Its
    counterintuitive secret is poor soil: nutrients cycle so fast that almost nothing is
    stored in the ground. The wealth is in the living biomass, which is why cleared
    rainforest turns to hardpan within a few seasons — the fertility was the forest, not
    the dirt.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="two strategies, one sunlight — drag between them" widget="compare" config={ecoForestCompare} />
  <p>
    <strong>Temperate deciduous forest</strong> trades the endless growing season for winter
    dormancy: leaves are dropped annually, their compost builds rich brown soils, and the
    forest supports fewer species but larger standing biomass per species.
    <strong>Boreal forest</strong> (taiga) runs cold and slow: evergreen needles give a
    running start each short summer, acidic litter slows decay, and soil fungi do the
    decomposition work that cold bacteria cannot — making it the slowest and most
    carbon-hoarding of the three, which is why boreal peat and permafrost are such outsized
    carbon stores.
  </p>
  <p>
    All three matter to the global carbon ledger: forests hold roughly 450–650 gigatonnes
    of carbon in biomass and soils, and deforestation releases about 1–1.5 gigatonnes a
    year — a tenth of the fossil-fuel problem, in one land-use decision.
  </p>

  <h2>The decomposer economy: nutrient cycles</h2>
  <p>
    The pipeline's leakiness only works because nothing material is lost. Decomposers are
    the recycling plant: bacteria and fungi secrete enzymes that digest dead tissue and
    break it into soluble molecules that roots reabsorb. Without them the world would drown
    in undecayed cellulose and the nutrient supply would jam within years. Fungal threads
    also trade directly with living trees — minerals for sugar — knitting whole forests
    into one underground marketplace.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two cycles deserve special mention. The <strong>nitrogen cycle</strong>: atmospheric
    N₂ is 78% of the air, yet its strong triple bond makes it inert — unusable by plants.
    <strong>Nitrogen-fixing</strong> bacteria crack it into ammonium and nitrate:
    free-living ones in soil, and <em>Rhizobium</em> in the root nodules of beans and
    clover — the reason farmers rotate legumes. Other bacteria run the return leg,
    converting nitrogen compounds back to N₂. The <strong>carbon cycle</strong> couples
    every meal to the atmosphere: photosynthesis pulls carbon in, respiration releases it,
    and the gap between those flows — carbon buried faster than it is respired — is what
    this subject's climate lessons track.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> plants breathe in nitrogen gas from the air and use it
  directly — after all, N₂ is 78% of the atmosphere and leaves exchange gases freely. But
  the triple bond makes N₂ inert to everything in a plant's chemistry: usable nitrogen
  arrives only after bacteria (or lightning, or fertiliser factories) have fixed it into
  ammonium and nitrate in the soil. Crop rotation with legumes is farming the bacteria,
  not the air.</p>

  <h2>Biodiversity: richness, evenness and keystones</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Biodiversity</strong> is measured on two axes. <em>Species richness</em> is how
    many kinds live somewhere; <em>evenness</em> is how fairly individuals are spread among
    them — a forest of 10 species in equal numbers is more diverse than one where 95% of
    individuals belong to a single species. Richness climbs predictably from the poles
    toward the tropics and from small areas to large ones, which is why conservation
    attention concentrates where it does.
  </p>
  <p>
    Why protect it? Three honest answers. Ecosystems with more species tend to be more
    productive and more stable — diversity is insurance against shocks. Biodiversity is our
    pharmacy and our crop gene pool. And the loss is permanent: extinction has no undo.
  </p>
  <p>
    Not all species are equal in influence. <strong>Keystone species</strong> punch far
    above their energy budget. Remove wolves from Yellowstone (as happened by 1926) and elk
    overgraze the riverbanks, collapsing willows, beavers and songbird habitat;
    reintroduce them in 1995 and the vegetation — and everything depending on it — begins
    to return. Such chain reactions are called <em>trophic cascades</em>. Sea otters hunting
    sea urchins protect kelp forests; a single starfish species structures whole intertidal
    zones (Paine's classic removal experiments). Management, then, is not "more of
    everything" but knowing which interactions hold the system together.
  </p>
  <p>
    The standard toolkit has four parts: protected areas sized with population viability in
    mind; corridors reconnecting fragmented habitat; species-specific programmes (captive
    breeding, CITES trade bans for elephants and rhinos); and community-based use —
    ecotourism and sustainable harvest, on the logic that a forest worth money standing
    survives better than one worth money felled. Effort concentrates in
    <strong>biodiversity hotspots</strong>: regions with huge numbers of endemic species
    that have already lost most of their original habitat (Madagascar, the tropical Andes,
    Southeast Asia), where a conservation budget buys the most extinctions prevented. The
    next lesson in this unit takes up these tools in detail.
  </p>

  <h2>Fieldwork: how ecologists actually count</h2>
  <p>
    Ecology is measurable, and the measurement tools are simple enough to use yourself. A
    <strong>quadrat</strong> — a square frame, often 1 m², placed at random positions —
    lets you estimate abundance: count individuals per quadrat and scale up by area, or
    record percentage cover for plants too numerous to count. Use many quadrats, never one:
    the average of many random samples is the honest estimate, and an honest error range
    goes with it.
  </p>
  <p>
    A <strong>transect</strong> samples along a line instead, capturing change across a
    gradient — a seashore's zonation from barnacles to bladderwrack, or a woodland edge
    fading into deep shade. Quadrats answer "how many here?"; transects answer "what
    changes, in which direction?".
  </p>
  <p>
    Mobile animals need <strong>mark–recapture</strong>: catch a sample, mark and release
    them, catch again. If you marked 40 beetles and your second catch of 30 contains 10
    marked, then marked/unmarked in the second sample mirrors the whole population:
    10/30 = 40/N, so N ≈ 120. The catch: marked animals must mix back in, and marks must
    not change behaviour — beetles painted bright red get eaten selectively, and the
    estimate collapses.
  </p>

  <h2>Succession: ecosystems have a growth arc</h2>
  <p>
    Left alone, an ecosystem builds itself in a predictable sequence called
    <strong>succession</strong>. On bare rock — after a lava flow or a retreating glacier —
    the first arrivals are <em>pioneer species</em>: lichens, fungus–alga partnerships that
    need no soil because they dissolve rock directly. Their dead matter, with windblown
    dust, makes the first thin soil; mosses take over; then grasses, shrubs and
    fast-growing trees, each stage trapping more water and organic matter, until a
    <em>climax community</em> — the tall forest the local climate allows — stabilises the
    system. After a disturbance that leaves soil intact (a fire, a cleared field), the same
    arc runs from grass instead of lichen, and much faster: <em>secondary</em> succession,
    the reason an abandoned field becomes woodland within decades.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two terms describe how an ecosystem handles disturbance. <em>Resistance</em> is its
    ability to keep functioning while the disturbance happens; <em>resilience</em> is the
    speed of recovery afterwards. A mature forest resists a drought better than a young
    one, but a grassland — built to burn and regrow — is often more resilient. And the
    energy logic ties back to the pyramid: each successional stage captures more of the
    incoming sunlight (higher NPP) and stores more of it in standing biomass. A forest is
    the most complete answer a landscape can give to the question of what to do with a
    photon.
  </p>

  <h2>The pyramid on your plate</h2>
  <p>
    The ten-percent rule scales to dinner. Livestock are mid-pipeline converters: a beef
    animal returns roughly a tenth of the plant energy it eats as meat — feed-conversion
    ratios run around 10 kg of feed per 1 kg of beef, with pork around 4:1 and chicken
    around 2:1. The consequence is a land-use multiplier. One classic calculation puts it
    starkly: 1,350 kilograms of grain and soybeans can support one person if first converted
    into beef — or 22 people if eaten directly. Producing the same protein from plants
    (pulses, grains, soy) typically needs many times less land and a fraction of the water,
    because it eats at the pipeline's base instead of one level up.
  </p>
  <p>
    This arithmetic — not ethics alone — is behind the environmental-science consensus that
    plant-rich diets are among the largest single levers on land use and agricultural
    emissions. It is the same curve you explored in the first widget, run in agricultural
    quantities: every trophic level you climb costs a factor of ten, and civilisation
    currently eats near the top.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>State the two rules every ecosystem obeys, and explain why they are stated separately.</li>
    <li>A food chain runs grass → rabbit → fox. If the grass fixes 100,000 units of energy, roughly how much reaches the fox — and where did the rest go?</li>
    <li>What is the difference between gross and net primary productivity, and which one can a herbivore actually eat?</li>
    <li>You mark and release 25 beetles; your second catch of 20 contains 5 marked. Estimate the population, and name one assumption the estimate depends on.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Matter cycles; energy flows. They are separate rules
    because heat dissipated at each transfer cannot loop back to the producers, while carbon
    and nitrogen genuinely do return — the ecosystem is open to energy and closed to matter.
    (2) About 1,000 units: two transfers of roughly 10% each. The rest was burned in
    respiration and movement, voided in waste, locked in indigestible parts, and much of it
    passed to decomposers — degraded to heat, not destroyed. (3) GPP is the total energy
    fixed by photosynthesis; NPP is what remains after the plants' own respiration — and
    NPP is the edible part. (4) 5/20 = 25/N, so N ≈ 100. It assumes the marked beetles mixed
    back in randomly, that the marks did not change their survival or catchability, and
    that few beetles entered or left the area between catches.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>An ecosystem is a biotic community plus its abiotic environment. Matter cycles; energy flows — two different rules.</li>
    <li>Producers capture only about 1% of incident sunlight; NPP (GPP minus plant respiration) is the surplus that feeds everything else.</li>
    <li>About 90% of energy is lost at each trophic handoff — to heat, movement, waste, indigestible parts and decomposers. Hence the ten-percent rule.</li>
    <li>The leaky pipeline explains why food chains are short, why top predators are rare and wide-ranging, and why plant-rich diets cost far less land.</li>
    <li>NPP varies by two orders of magnitude across biomes — rainforest around 2,200 g/m²/yr, desert under 100, open ocean about 125 but over a vast area.</li>
    <li>Biodiversity has two axes (richness and evenness); keystone species hold food webs together, and removing them triggers trophic cascades.</li>
    <li>Fieldwork: quadrats for abundance, transects for change along gradients, mark–recapture for mobile populations.</li>
    <li>Succession rebuilds ecosystems from pioneer species toward a climax community; resistance and resilience describe how systems face disturbance.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    This lesson gave you the energy skeleton of ecosystems; the next lesson in this unit,
    Biodiversity and conservation, adds the flesh — how biologists measure diversity in the
    field and how the toolkit of reserves, corridors and community programmes plays out in
    real conflicts. The carbon ledger you met in the forest section returns in the climate
    lessons, where the same cycles decide how much carbon dioxide the land and ocean can
    absorb — and for how long.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The ten-percent rule is a pedagogical honest lie: measured transfer efficiencies run
    from 5% to 20%, and the biggest systematic difference is warm-bloodedness. Cold-blooded
    animals (ectotherms) do not burn food to stay warm, so they convert far more of what
    they eat into biomass — a caterpillar's net production efficiency has been measured at
    about 18%, a squirrel's at about 1.6%. Warm-bloodedness buys activity in the cold and
    pays for it in groceries: endotherms must eat far more often. Real ecosystems are also
    <em>food webs</em>, not chains — omnivory, detrital pathways and microbial loops criss-
    cross the pyramid — which is why ecosystem modelling moved from Lindeman's neat
    diagrams to full energy-flow matrices.
  </p>
  <p>
    The founder of the method, Raymond Lindeman, died at 27 before seeing his landmark 1942
    study of Silver Springs in print — and the paper had been rejected as "interesting but
    prematurely published"; the referees thought quantifying ecology was a generation too
    early. It instead founded an entire discipline. The modern frontier pushes in two
    directions: upward into <em>ecosystem services</em> — the Millennium Ecosystem
    Assessment's four-way split (provisioning, regulating, cultural, supporting) now
    underlies how the world prices pollination, flood control and carbon storage — and
    downward into the soil microbiome, where the majority of species are unnamed and the
    deepest carbon stores live. The pipeline metaphor holds; what keeps surprising us is
    how much of the flow runs through pipes nobody has mapped.
  </p>

